#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c67f5c8-ace8-5d01-a0dd-292ecf67870e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309706} {:answer/id #uuid "594a6210-b380-585c-bf83-0bf3c729789b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309707} {:answer/id #uuid "de2b02ea-174b-5122-9453-c66727e62db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309708} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ef221d-834b-52cb-8252-7ccbb7b48aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309709} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc4b75e-26da-5fcb-b923-3c0df7170a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ef221d-834b-52cb-8252-7ccbb7b48aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309709} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7847158-31d8-5468-ac51-329fb39a9f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65b81769-10fa-5dce-86a3-3a97e46cc0ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-140459" :question/problem "Given that $‖5u-4v‖=26$ and $‖u‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖v‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖5u-4v‖ & \\le ‖5u‖+‖-4v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖5u-4v‖ & \\le 5‖u‖+4‖v‖ \\\\ 26 & \\le 5\\cdot 2+4‖v‖ \\\\ 26 & \\le 10+4‖v‖ \\\\ 16 & \\le 4‖v‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖v‖$ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f8016f-6ef7-57c4-8ffa-937e9afa02d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131343} {:answer/id #uuid "01b1041b-b79e-57d5-95e7-f4b05974c395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131344} {:answer/id #uuid "497f1cd8-8f6f-5fd9-b0f3-a5bcd8b9ef29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131345} {:answer/id #uuid "66e27020-a003-5e9f-b39e-dd0ec7a7b70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131346} {:answer/id #uuid "7e51aebc-db57-5617-a11b-53d382973fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e51aebc-db57-5617-a11b-53d382973fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131347} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a565d2f-881d-59ff-83e9-7acd86e5fbdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0481f3fc-bbad-5359-bc8f-155c905afe87" :question/math-academy-id "q-39610" :question/problem "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2}=50$ and $‖u‖=‖w‖=4$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =17‖v{‖}^{2}+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & \\le 17‖v‖+16 \\\\ 34 & \\le 17‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $2\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{2}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d37c882f-e260-5a29-8c97-9a35b7408443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309783} {:answer/id #uuid "d05d7d9c-3ce0-5045-8952-1c3c67011f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309784} {:answer/id #uuid "1f6dfc9f-ef8f-54bb-8608-2c5733692844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309785} {:answer/id #uuid "04d8a5c1-d829-542e-9206-9951103bfa62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309786} {:answer/id #uuid "6e07238e-ae6f-51bf-8644-94ef27697c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d37c882f-e260-5a29-8c97-9a35b7408443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309783} :answer-field/id #uuid "82fbf3b5-a6c7-5638-a0ea-2990e64c070d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd88faa5-6854-51de-a79d-d62db631781d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74191" :question/problem "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2}=62$ and $‖u‖=‖w‖=3$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62 & ={|u\\cdot v|}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9‖v{‖}^{2}+4‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+4(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+4(‖v{‖}^{2}+{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =13‖v{‖}^{2}+36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62 & \\le 13‖v{‖}^{2}+36 \\\\ 26 & \\le 13‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $2\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{2}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}]}
